# Embodiment

Imagine for a moment, a seafaring sailing vessel. It’s a place some people call their home. It travels around the world, yet in itself is a place. It has an interior with various rooms with different purposes, and to its residents it is their home, an emblem of their dignity.

The human body is much like this. It has an exterior that is both practical and beautiful, a perfectly imperfect stone created by God. Like the sailing vessel, it has an interior: the sweeping Gothic arches of the lungs, adorned with alveoli like respiratory cathedrals; the winding cloisters of the ear canal; or the catacombs of the sinuses.

Your body is a vessel, and like a ship, in a sense it has its own intelligence, built from distinct communities of trillions of cells self-organizing into organs that respectively specialize and distribute the load of cellular respiration that grants the metabolic headspace for cognition, advanced motor function, and environmental resilience.

In this moment I contemplate what kernel of truth is most important to share with you, dear reader.

Listen to your body carefully, but be careful not to confuse the anxious neuroticism of a nervous system with threat detection turned up to 11 with legitimate signals from different parts of the body.

In Western culture so much emphasis is put on reason. We spend so much attention on our minds that it is sometimes difficult to listen to the body. Because in order to listen to the body, you have to quiet the mind and move your attention away from your thoughts, away from your mind, and use your attention to scan your body and inhabit different parts of it.

Such practice can give you deeper insight into ailments, can help guide you in what medicine or food your body needs. It is an incredibly powerful skill if you can hone your ability to tell the difference between clean signal and hallucination.

This is the first thing to practice: tuning in with your body so you don’t injure yourself, and so you can gain deeper insight into ailments you may be struggling with.

Beyond bodily consciousness there are a few domains we need to briefly touch and get to the core principle of: nutrition, fasting and cleansing practices, exercise, embodiment practices, how the body remembers trauma, basic wound care, the basics of practicing practical pharmacology, breathing, posture, choosing health practitioners to collaborate with.


Water

Water is the medium through which information travels and metabolic reactions take place. Blood, lymph, cytosol, extracellular fluid—the machinery of the body operates in an aqueous environment. Ions move through it, nutrients and waste products are transported through it, proteins fold and interact in it, and basically every metabolic pathway depends on the chemistry of that environment.

Water is the foundation of nutrition, so it is one of the first things you should try to get right in perfecting embodiment.

Firstly, drink enough of it. A simple rule-of-thumb diagnostic is that most of the time your urine should be somewhere between pale yellow and clear. You do not need to piss distilled water all day. You are looking for the general pattern. Even relatively minor hypohydration can measurably fuck with attention and cognitive performance, alongside the obvious effects on thermoregulation and physical performance.

The next thing to understand is that the water you drink is not merely H₂O.

Water is a solvent. By the time it reaches you it contains a chemical history of where it came from and what it passed through: dissolved minerals, salts, metals, organic molecules and potentially whatever contaminants entered the groundwater, treatment system, plumbing or container.

That matters because water is not some occasional exposure. You drink liters of it, every day, for decades.

A water supply can satisfy somebody's definition of safe and still not be the water you would deliberately design for yourself. "Safe" answers a particular regulatory or toxicological question under a particular set of assumptions. I am interested in a slightly different question: if this is going to be one of the largest material inputs into my body for the next fifty years, what chemistry do I actually want it to have?

Groundwater is not something I would trust by default. It is essentially an extract of the geology underneath you combined with whatever human activity has happened above it. Arsenic alone varies enormously between aquifers; studies of private wells in known high-arsenic regions have found substantial fractions exceeding drinking-water limits, and groundwater quality can also change with season and local conditions.

If you drink groundwater, get it tested. Get the actual concentrations, not merely somebody telling you that it "passed." Look at what is in it and research the implications yourself. Maybe your well is beautiful. Maybe it isn't. Measure the thing.

The easiest solution for most people is much less romantic.

Buy a five-gallon glass carboy and a dispenser. Once a week, take the carboy somewhere with a properly maintained reverse-osmosis refill system and fill it. This is cheap, you do not have to own or maintain the RO system yourself, and you now have a nearly blank starting material whose mineral profile you can rebuild deliberately.

RO removes the overwhelming majority of calcium and magnesium along with a great deal of the material you actually wanted removed, so post-RO remineralization is a perfectly sensible operation. Experimental work on RO-produced drinking water also shows that stripping the mineral content changes the character of the water considerably and that remineralization with calcium and magnesium changes it back.

Then rebuild it.

Do not get hypnotized by pH.

There is a tremendous amount of hype around alkaline water. The number printed on the bottle tells you surprisingly little by itself. pH is a logarithmic measurement of hydrogen-ion activity; it is not a measurement of the total amount of acid or base present.

Nearly demineralized water can swing several pH units after receiving a tiny amount of acid or base simply because there is almost nothing in it to buffer the change.

What I care about more is mineral composition and buffering capacity.

Your body already operates an elaborate acid-base machine. The stomach secretes hydrochloric acid. The pancreas supplies bicarbonate downstream. Blood uses a carbon dioxide/bicarbonate buffering system, while respiration and renal function continuously regulate it. Drinking bicarbonate-rich water can measurably change renal acid handling and urinary chemistry, demonstrating that the relevant variable is the actual chemical load and not merely whether a bottle says pH 9.

So rather than chasing some magical pH, rebuild the mineral system and allow the resulting pH to emerge from that chemistry.

The basic constituents are not complicated:

Calcium.
Magnesium.
Bicarbonate.
Some potassium.
A smidge of sodium and chloride.

That is most of what I care about.

And remember that ppm sums. We are building one solution, not choosing five independent numbers from five different recommendation ranges.

As a concrete example, a lightly mineralized reconstructed water could land around:

15 ppm calcium
8 ppm magnesium
2 ppm potassium
3 ppm sodium

If the calcium, magnesium and potassium are predominantly balanced by bicarbonate and the sodium is supplied as a small amount of sodium chloride, that works out to roughly 89 ppm bicarbonate, about 4.6 ppm chloride, and around 122 ppm total major dissolved ions.

The bicarbonate in that example corresponds to roughly 73 ppm alkalinity expressed as CaCO₃.

That is a coherent water.

It is not an established biological optimum handed down by God. It is a useful engineering target for lightly rebuilding stripped RO water without making the mineral load enormous. For comparison, controlled sensory work on remineralized RO water tested total dissolved solids from less than 5 to 440 mg/L and found the strongest perception of freshness considerably higher, around 190–350 mg/L. So something around 120 ppm is still fairly modest water.

Magnesium is particularly worth paying attention to. It participates in ATP-dependent metabolism, enzymatic reactions, neuromuscular function and membrane physiology, and magnesium dissolved in water is not merely decorative. Controlled human work shows that magnesium in mineralized water is readily bioavailable, with its absorption affected by the accompanying mineral chemistry and by whether it is consumed with food.

Calcium is also absorbed from water. Human isotope studies have demonstrated that clearly. But calcium is relatively easy to obtain from food, as is sodium chloride, so I see little reason to make either of them the dominant feature of reconstructed water.

Potassium is harder to obtain in absurd excess from the normal modern diet and is one of the principal intracellular ions governing osmotic balance, membrane potential and electrical activity. You only need a little in the water. The water is not supposed to carry your entire daily potassium intake.

Commercial remineralization products already do most of this. Many use calcium and magnesium carbonate or related mineral systems. Find one whose composition you can actually determine rather than buying something covered in mystical "72 trace minerals" marketing bullshit.

Then measure what you made.

A cheap EC or conductivity meter is useful here, not because conductivity tells you whether the water is good, but because once you know the composition of a recipe that you like, conductivity becomes a cheap batch-control measurement.

You formulate the water once.

You measure it.

Now next week's carboy should behave approximately like this week's carboy.

Purify.
Rebuild.
Measure.
Repeat.

There are plenty of other trace constituents found naturally in water—silicon, boron, selenium, lithium and others—and some of them appear to participate in interesting biological side reactions. They are bonuses. They are not the foundation. Get the major ions right first.

The container matters too. I like glass precisely because there is not much to say about it. Put the rebuilt water in a glass carboy, keep the carboy clean, dispense it, refill it next week.

Clean, modestly remineralized water also becomes an excellent substrate for everything else. Herbal teas. Electrolytes. Magnesium citrate. Broth. Liquid supplements. Whatever gentle intervention you are using at the time.

This is the larger principle I am pointing at.

Do not neglect the substrate.

You can spend enormous amounts of money trying to push physiology around at the margins while ignoring several liters of material flowing through the system every day.

If you are chronically dehydrated, anything else you are doing to improve your health is working uphill.
